# The second brief, answered the obvious way: a line, and a drive train for it.
#
# The whole lesson of this design is in two words on one component. A crusher
# will not take a drive turning at speed 6, and a mill will not take one turning
# at speed 1, so a motor cannot drive both. Two trains, one geared down and one
# not, and the gearbox is the only part of the machine that does nothing to the
# ore.
#
# The mill is the bottleneck and says so: two motors on one drive give it 67
# rotary of the 80 it wants, so the line runs at 80/tick rather than 100 and
# everything upstream backs up behind it.
#
# "Two motors on one drive" used to be two motors bolted to one line shaft, and
# the shaft is gone. Nothing about the sharing went with it -- a port takes as
# many wires as you draw into it and divides what arrives -- which is the
# shortest statement of why the transport family was deleted. The shaft was
# never what let six machines hang off one engine. Fan-out was.
#
# What did go with it is the defect this file was kept unfixed to display.
# Experiment 10's router refused `MO2.rotary -> SH1.in`, because both motors
# were bolted to the same end of the same shaft and only one of them can be: a
# coupling is a straight line between two flanges, MO1 was already on that
# line, and MO2 was three and a half metres south of it. There is no shaft now,
# so each motor gets its own straight line to the gearbox and both of them can
# have one. The refusal was real and the router was right; it was a fact about
# a component that no longer exists.

machine "Crush Line"
brief crush

inlet     I1  at 0,4   draws ore

crusher   C1  at 0,0
crusher   C2  at 4,0
mill      MI1 at 9,0
separator S1  at 14,0
outlet    O1  at 19,0
skip      SK1 at 19,3

gearbox   GB1 at 0,7   ratio 4

mains     M1  at 10,9
motor     MO1 at 6,9
motor     MO2 at 6,12
motor     MO3 at 14,9
motor     MO4 at 14,12

wire M1.power -> MO1.power
wire M1.power -> MO2.power
wire M1.power -> MO3.power
wire M1.power -> MO4.power

wire MO1.rotary -> GB1.in
wire MO2.rotary -> GB1.in
wire GB1.out -> C1.drive
wire GB1.out -> C2.drive

wire MO3.rotary -> MI1.drive
wire MO4.rotary -> MI1.drive
wire MO3.rotary -> S1.drive
wire MO4.rotary -> S1.drive

wire I1.out -> C1.in
wire C1.out -> C2.in
wire C2.out -> MI1.in
wire MI1.out -> S1.in
wire S1.rich -> O1.solid
wire S1.tails -> SK1.solid
